{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And Miriam the prophetess took.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): it says <b>the sister of Aharon</b> so that all three siblings — Moshe, Aharon, and Miriam — would be mentioned in this song; and further, because it is the way of Scripture to attribute a person to the greatest of the siblings, in the manner of what is written (Genesis 36:22): ״ואחות לוטן תמנע״. And it is also possible to say that it mentioned 'the sister of Aharon' in order to bring it into proximity with the timbrel, which corresponds to צדק, and it is written of Aharon (Job 29:14): ״צדק לבשתי וילבשני״. As you already know, in the psalm (Psalms 150:1) ״הללו אל בקדשו״ there are ten praises and there are in it nine types of music, aligned, for the discerning, corresponding to the nine spheres; the timbrel corresponds to צדק, and the dance to מאדים, and this is why it says 'Aharon' [in proximity to] the timbrel." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'the prophetess, the sister of Aharon' — and not the sister of Moshe; rather, this teaches that she prophesied at the time when she was [only] the sister of Aharon — [foretelling] that her mother was destined to give birth to a son who would deliver Israel. End quote." "<b>And as for</b> the fact that prophecy rests upon a woman — it appears proper to say that one should not marvel at this, since she is of the human species and is called אדם, as it is stated (Genesis 5:2): ״ויקרא את שמם אדם״. <b>And behold,</b> from this they said that Sarah was a prophetess like Miriam or greater than she, for she is called Yiskah on account of her being סוכה in the holy spirit, and our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Genesis 21:12): ״כל אשר תאמר אליך שרה״ וגו׳ — this teaches that Abraham was subordinate to Sarah in prophecy. But Scripture did not mention prophecy until Miriam — either for the reason of the Midrash that I have mentioned, or for the reason that it wished to wait until the time of the indwelling of the Shekhinah, concerning which our Rabbis of blessed memory said: 'A maidservant saw at the sea what Ezekiel did not see.' And it informs us that Miriam the prophetess praises the Shekhinah, and all the women after her, and she herself utters this song just as Moshe and the children of Israel did." "<b>And further,</b> you will find great principles of the Torah explicated through women — as in the matter of the World to Come, called צרור החיים, through Avigayil; and the matter of the resurrection of the dead and the orders of supplication through Hannah; and the matter of gilgul through the woman of Tekoa. And all this demonstrates that a woman is not an entirely subordinate thing, but she has essential standing. And our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded in tractate Megillah: 'Seven prophetesses there were, and these are they: Sarah, Miriam, Hannah, Devorah, Huldah, Avigayil, and Esther' — and this corresponds to the seven sefirot of reception, and this is clear." "<b>With timbrels and with dances.</b> These women were great in wisdom, as our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: 'A maidservant saw at the sea what Ezekiel did not see.' And among them were those whose intention was toward Heaven, and among them were those whose intention was evil — therefore they went out with dances. As you already know, the dance corresponds to מאדים, and from this you will find in the episode of the calf (Exodus 32:19): ״וירא את העגל ומחולות״, and there I will elaborate on this with the help of the Holy One, blessed be He."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:59:56.698314787Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}